[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Nested Tests and Hierarchical Test Structures (@Nested) in JUnit 5"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Hierarchical Testing"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"As test suites grow in size and complexity, organizing tests flatly inside a single class becomes unmaintainable. JUnit 5 introduces the @Nested annotation, which allows developers to create hierarchical test structures. This pattern helps group tests logically based on the state of the system under test, mirroring the natural domain hierarchy."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use @Nested Tests?"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Readability: Tests for specific methods or conditions are grouped together, making the test report read like a specification."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shared Setup/Teardown: Inner classes can have their own @BeforeEach and @AfterEach lifecycle methods, inheriting or overriding outer setups."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Contextual Grouping: Ideal for testing objects that have distinct states, such as a ShoppingCart in an 'Empty' state versus a 'Non-Empty' state."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Syntax and Implementation"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A nested test class must be a non-static inner class. The @Nested annotation tells the JUnit 5 TestEngine to traverse into the inner class and execute its tests alongside the outer tests."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class StackTest {

    Stack<String> stack;

    @Test
    void isInstantiatedWithNoElements() {
        stack = new Stack<>();
        assertTrue(stack.isEmpty());
    }

    @Nested
    class WhenNew {

        @BeforeEach
        void createNewStack() {
            stack = new Stack<>();
        }

        @Test
        void isInitiallyEmpty() {
            assertTrue(stack.isEmpty());
        }

        @Test
        void throwsExceptionWhenPopped() {
            assertThrows(EmptyStackException.class, stack::pop);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Lifecycle Methods in Hierarchical Structures"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lifecycle methods execute hierarchically. For any test inside a @Nested class, the @BeforeEach and @AfterEach methods of the outer class run first, followed by the corresponding methods of the inner class. This allows you to progressively build up the test state from general to specific."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Hierarchical Testing"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Name inner classes clearly, often starting with 'When', 'With', or 'Without' to describe the specific context."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid excessive nesting (more than 3 levels deep), as it can degrade code readability rather than improve it."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine @Nested with @DisplayName to generate clean, human-readable test reports in your CI/CD pipelines."}]
